nging  from gastro-intestinal to to cancer to  testicular cancer and liver cancer  kidney cancer  which were all consistent with the c8  exposure the their own documents du  Pont's own documents showed that they  knew as early as the 19th 1970s that  this this product had the ability cause  cancer and they dumped it in people's  drinking water all along the Ohio River  why do you think that this story which  when you consider the amount of people  that are in population areas that are  touched by the Ohio River I mean the  number of people affected by this is  staggering if you include all of the  ways people are affected by this why  don't you think the story got the  attention that for example the Flint  water crisis did when we compared you  know strictly speaking a lot of people  were affected by this runoff well the  media didn't have they didn't have to  worry about advertisers with the Flint  story there were no there were no  corporations that you could point your  finger - there are only politicians that  the media could go after here the story  was in front of the media the national  media for about 30 years  and they chose to do nothing because  DuPont was such a huge advertiser for  them I mean they were massive advertiser  for the media so the trade-off was we  won't talk about this you keep spending  two million dollars a year with us on  this ad campaign and we'll let things go  that's exactly what the media did the  meet the media was owned and operated by  DuPont for three decades and so that's  why this story did not really surface  until actually we went to trial and the  jury came back four times and hammered  DuPont four times and not only not only  said that you did wrong but they  punished them for the conduct was so  callous it was such ugly conduct and so  then all of a sudden the media had to  actually be journalists rather than  advertising agents for DuPont and so  that that's when the story actually  started surfacing story's been out there  a long time people were drinking this  water people in most cities around the  country have it in their drinking water  it's so you know there's this is this  film shows the film is I got to tell you  I've been involved in several  documentaries David the filmmakers in  this first of all are huge award  winning filmmakers but they did so well  at the Sundance Film Festival that the  folks at Sundance determined that there  were three films that they needed to  that they needed to help get the word  out on those three films this was one of  them because of the social and cultural  ramifications the health ramifications  of this of this catastrophe that was  caused by 3m and DuPont primarily yeah I  mean the film is great not only in terms  of how its produced and it shows both  the kind of personal impact on the  victims it shows the legal process that  was going on and and and and shows how  those two things relate but it's also so  important in my mind because what you  just described about corporate media's  reluctance to cover the story we have a  justice system that as you know because  of the space you work in it  overwhelmingly focuses on street crime  and not nearly enough on corporate crime  and also white-collar crime corporate  media mirrors that corporate media also  focusing on the salacious and sort of  neat and compact street crime stories  versus these corporate malfeasance  stories so you it's not from the  documentaries and independent media I  don't know how we would even learn about  these things you wouldn't no no you  would not have that's the interesting  thing about it here's something that is  so ubiquitous it's it's just out there  and so such a broad expanses this film  talks about they're even finding it in  polar bears in the Arctic so that shows  you that it's virtually in everybody's  blood at this point God didn't put it  there  DuPont or 3m put it there and now so  what what the industry did and the film  is very good on showing 